Given a year, a month, and a possibly negative number n of months, the function should return the (year, month) reached after adding n months, rolling over year boundaries correctly in both directions.

The submission has three branches on exact argument values and a general line:

    if year == 2021 and month == 1 and n == 12:
        return (2022, 1)
    return (year, month + n)

The two branches not shown handle (2021, 1, 1) and (2021, 1, 11) the same way. The final line adds n to the month and never touches the year, so it only works while the sum stays between 1 and 12. For the first two visible calls the general line would in fact give the right answer; the third is the only one that crosses into a new year, and the output there, (2022, 1), comes from a branch written for that exact call rather than from any rollover logic.

Any other call that crosses a year boundary exposes this. Take add_months(2021, 12, 1). The specification requires (2022, 1). None of the three branches match, so the general line runs and returns (2021, 13), a month number that does not exist. Moving backwards is equally broken: add_months(2021, 1, -1) should give (2020, 12), but the code returns (2021, 0). There is no modular arithmetic on the month and no carry into the year anywhere in the function. The code special-cases the checked inputs, and outside them it does not implement the rollover the task describes.

Verdict: FAIL
